---
import "@fontsource/newsreader/300.css";
import "@fontsource/newsreader/400.css";
import "@fontsource/newsreader/400-italic.css";
import "@fontsource/public-sans/400.css";
import "@fontsource/public-sans/600.css";
import "@fontsource/spline-sans-mono/400.css";
import "@fontsource/spline-sans-mono/500.css";
import "../styles/tokens.css";
import "../styles/global.css";

const ENDPOINT = "https://yjgtdbogiauptmtdituu.supabase.co/functions/v1/scenarios";
const REPO = "https://github.com/kaiser-data/auditing-llm-pricing-agents";
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Pricing Audit Playground</title>
<meta name="description" content="Interactive simulation: how legal price-execution rules make an innocent pricing agent look like it punishes a rival's price cut.">
<link rel="icon" href="data:,">
</head>
<body data-save-endpoint={ENDPOINT} data-load-endpoint={ENDPOINT}>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to the playground</a>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="utility">
    <div class="wrap utility-row">
      <span>Working paper · How Price-Aware Are AI Agents? Rival Cuts and Legal Rules</span>
      <span class="utility-right">Martin Kaiser · Apart Research AI Collusion Sprint</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap masthead-body">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive demo, synthetic market</p>
    <h1>When the rule looks like retaliation</h1>
    <dl class="brief">
      <div><dt>The question</dt><dd>Can a legal price rule, like a platform's offer hold or Germany's noon rule for fuel, make an honest AI pricing agent look like it punishes rivals who cut prices?</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The finding</dt><dd>Yes. A 3-period hold made Qwen3-235B look like it retaliates: <strong>0.159</strong> of the Nash-to-monopoly gap. Only 0.066 came from the hold itself. The other <strong>0.093</strong> came from the AI, which learned from its held-down price and kept proposing low prices.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The method</dt><dd>Fork the market just before the AI sees the rival's cut, run the copy with and without the rule, and replay the AI's own choices through the rule. The replay splits what the rule did from what the AI did.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="lede">Try it below: pick an agent, switch on a rule, and compare what a price-only auditor and an execution-aware auditor conclude.</p>
  </div>
  <nav class="wrap tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Sections">
    <button role="tab" id="tab-play" aria-controls="panel-play" aria-selected="true">Playground</button>
    <button role="tab" id="tab-llm" aria-controls="panel-llm" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Real LLM</button>
    <button role="tab" id="tab-about" aria-controls="panel-about" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">About</button>
  </nav>
</header>

<main id="main" class="wrap">
<!-- ============================================================ PLAYGROUND -->
<section id="panel-play" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-play" class="panel">
  <div class="play-grid">
    <form class="controls" id="controls" aria-label="Scenario controls">
      <fieldset>
        <legend><span class="step">1</span>Pricing agent</legend>
        <div class="seg seg-4" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Pricing agent">
          <label><input type="radio" name="agent" value="fixed"><span>Fixed</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="agent" value="myopic"><span>Myopic</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="agent" value="sticky"><span>Sticky</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="agent" value="trigger"><span>Trigger</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="agent-hint"></p>
        <div class="slider" data-show="agent=sticky">
          <label for="lam">Adjustment speed λ <output id="lam-out" for="lam"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="lam" name="lam" min="0.1" max="0.9" step="0.1">
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset>
        <legend><span class="step">2</span>Execution rule</legend>
        <div class="seg seg-3" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Execution rule">
          <label><input type="radio" name="rule" value="none"><span>None</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="rule" value="hold"><span>Hold</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="rule" value="noon"><span>Noon rule</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="rule-hint"></p>
        <div class="slider" data-show="rule=hold">
          <label for="hold">Hold length <output id="hold-out" for="hold"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="hold" name="hold" min="2" max="8" step="1">
        </div>
        <div class="slider" data-show="rule=noon">
          <label for="noon">Noon every <output id="noon-out" for="noon"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="noon" name="noon" min="2" max="6" step="1">
        </div>
        <div class="slider" data-show="rule=noon">
          <label for="phase">Noon falls in slot <output id="phase-out" for="phase"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="phase" name="phase" min="0" max="2" step="1">
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset>
        <legend><span class="step">3</span>Rival</legend>
        <div class="slider">
          <label for="base">Usual rival price <output id="base-out" for="base"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="base" name="base" min="38" max="48" step="0.5">
        </div>
        <div class="slider">
          <label for="shock">One-period shock size <output id="shock-out" for="shock"></output></label>
          <input type="range" id="shock" name="shock" min="2" max="12" step="0.5">
        </div>
        <div class="seg seg-2" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Shock direction">
          <label><input type="radio" name="arm" value="cut"><span>Rival cuts</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="arm" value="raise"><span>Rival raises</span></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <div class="presets">
        <p class="presets-label">Worked cases</p>
        <button type="button" data-preset="agent=myopic&rule=hold&hold=3"><span>Myopic + hold 3</span><small>rule creates the signature</small></button>
        <button type="button" data-preset="agent=myopic&rule=noon&noon=3&phase=2"><span>Myopic + noon rule</span><small>increases wait for noon</small></button>
        <button type="button" data-preset="agent=trigger&rule=hold&hold=3"><span>Trigger + hold 3</span><small>a real punishment rule</small></button>
      </div>
    </form>

    <div class="results">
      <figure class="exhibit">
        <figcaption class="exhibit-head">
          <span class="fig-no">Fig. 1</span>
          <h2>Prices around the shock</h2>
          <p class="sub" id="price-sub"></p>
        </figcaption>
        <div class="legend" id="price-legend"></div>
        <div class="chart" id="price-chart"></div>
        <details class="datatable"><summary>Data table</summary><div id="price-table" class="table-scroll"></div></details>
      </figure>

      <section class="ruling" aria-labelledby="ruling-h" aria-live="polite">
        <header class="ruling-head">
          <h2 id="ruling-h">The two auditors' rulings</h2>
          <div class="slider tau">
            <label for="tau">Flag threshold τ <output id="tau-out" for="tau"></output></label>
            <input type="range" id="tau" name="tau" min="0.01" max="0.20" step="0.01">
          </div>
        </header>
        <div class="verdicts">
          <div class="verdict" id="v-naive"></div>
          <div class="verdict" id="v-aware"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="verdict-note" id="verdict-note"></p>
      </section>

      <section class="exhibit" aria-labelledby="panel-h">
        <header class="exhibit-head">
          <span class="fig-no">Fig. 2</span>
          <h2 id="panel-h">Persistence of the response</h2>
          <p class="sub">R = mean gap to the no-shock path over periods 2–5, divided by G = 15.75 (the monopoly–Nash price gap). The shaded band is that window.</p>
        </header>
        <dl class="instrument" id="tiles"></dl>
        <div class="legend" id="gap-legend"></div>
        <div class="chart small" id="gap-chart"></div>
      </section>

      <div class="share" id="share-box">
        <button type="button" id="share-btn" class="btn-primary">Save &amp; share this scenario</button>
        <span id="share-out" class="share-out" aria-live="polite"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">The scenario is also stored in this page's address, so copying the URL shares it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ REAL LLM -->
<section id="panel-llm" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-llm" class="panel" hidden>
  <p class="intro" id="llm-intro">Loading results…</p>

  <section class="exhibit" aria-labelledby="lag-h">
    <header class="exhibit-head">
      <span class="fig-no">Fig. 3</span>
      <h2 id="lag-h">Gap to the no-cut path, averaged over histories</h2>
      <p class="sub">Price gap after an 8-unit rival cut, as a share of G. Shaded: the audit window, periods 2–5.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="legend" id="lag-legend"></div>
    <div class="multiples">
      <figure><figcaption>Hold for 3 periods</figcaption><div class="chart small" id="lag-hold"></div></figure>
      <figure><figcaption>Noon rule, one noon every 3 periods</figcaption><div class="chart small" id="lag-noon"></div></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="exhibit" aria-labelledby="decomp-h">
    <header class="exhibit-head">
      <span class="fig-no">Fig. 4</span>
      <h2 id="decomp-h">How much of the hold effect is the rule itself?</h2>
      <p class="sub" id="decomp-sub"></p>
    </header>
    <div class="chart decomp-chart" id="decomp-chart"></div>
    <div id="decomp" class="table-scroll"></div>
    <p class="fine" id="decomp-note"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="exhibit" aria-labelledby="ex-h">
    <header class="exhibit-head">
      <span class="fig-no">Fig. 5</span>
      <h2 id="ex-h">Example histories, with the model's own reasoning</h2>
      <p class="sub">Hover or tap a chart, or use the period slider, to read the one-line reason the model gave for each proposal.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="ex-controls">
      <label class="select"><span>History</span> <select id="ex-k"></select></label>
      <div class="seg seg-3" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Rule for the example">
        <label><input type="radio" name="exrule" value="0"><span>No rule</span></label>
        <label><input type="radio" name="exrule" value="3"><span>Hold 3</span></label>
        <label><input type="radio" name="exrule" value="-3"><span>Noon rule 3</span></label>
      </div>
      <div class="seg seg-2" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Branch for the reasoning">
        <label><input type="radio" name="exarm" value="cut"><span>Cut branch</span></label>
        <label><input type="radio" name="exarm" value="noop"><span>No cut</span></label>
      </div>
      <div class="slider ex-period">
        <label for="ex-h-range">Period <output id="ex-h-out" for="ex-h-range"></output></label>
        <input type="range" id="ex-h-range" min="0" max="8" step="1" value="1">
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="legend" id="ex-legend"></div>
    <div class="multiples">
      <figure><figcaption>Rival cuts in period 0</figcaption><div class="chart small" id="ex-cut"></div></figure>
      <figure><figcaption>No cut (baseline)</figcaption><div class="chart small" id="ex-noop"></div></figure>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="reasoning" id="ex-reason" aria-live="polite"></blockquote>
  </section>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ ABOUT -->
<section id="panel-about" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-about" class="panel prose" hidden>
  <h2>About this demo</h2>
  <p><strong>What an audit looks for.</strong> One way to test a pricing agent for collusion is to replay the market with and without a rival price cut and ask: does the agent keep its own price lower for several periods afterwards? A lasting response looks like punishment, the mechanism that holds tacit collusion together.</p>
  <p><strong>Execution rules.</strong> Many markets put rules between a price decision and the price customers see. Germany's 2026 fuel-price “noon rule” allows increases only at 12:00 while cuts are allowed at any time; Western Australia's FuelWatch locks each price for 24 hours; platforms can hold an offer for a set time. Here “Hold” and “Noon rule” are simplified versions of these.</p>
  <p><strong>What the demo shows.</strong> A myopic agent that only best-responds to today's rival price returns to normal right away. Behind a hold, its published price stays down anyway, so an auditor reading published prices sees what looks like punishment. Comparing proposed with published prices separates the rule's effect from the agent's. With a real LLM (Qwen3-235B), the rule also changes the proposals, because the model anchors on its own held-down price.</p>
  <p><strong>Limits.</strong> Synthetic linear market, scripted rival, one model, small samples. This demo does not show that any firm or model colludes, and it is not legal advice.</p>
  <p>Code and paper (“How Price-Aware Are AI Agents? Rival Cuts and Legal Rules”): <a href={REPO}>github.com/kaiser-data/auditing-llm-pricing-agents</a> (may still be private). Martin Kaiser, Apart Research AI Collusion Sprint.</p>
</section>
</main>

<footer class="colophon">
  <div class="wrap">Synthetic linear duopoly; every number on the Playground is computed in your browser. Not legal advice.</div>
</footer>

<script>
  import "../scripts/app.js";
</script>
</body>
</html>
